Well, I am going to assume this is not a troll....

I can offer an opinion based on some years with AIX, and some
varying levels of experience with some of the other UNIXes, as
well as clones and derivatives...


First, some nits on terms.  You might be interested in looking at
www.unix-systems.org - they own the UNIX(TM) and you can see what
brand names are associated with what version of the, um, "standards".

You will note there that all the modern versions of the major unix
vendors can now be called UNIX(TM) and are _all_ _proprietary_.
That goes with the UNIX(TM).  They all have their strengths and
detractions - of AIX, Solaris, Digital UNIX (now True64) and HP-UX
(roughly listed in the order of my personal experience level) I
am most partial to AIX for admin tools and the fact that Journaled
file systems are not an option, but a given - system wide.  My
dealings with Digital UNIX with a couple of the 4.  versions (d and
e as I recall) left me with great hope, as some of the admin tools
might even be better in some respects - but as of those versions, 
journaled files systems seemed a bit of a mystery (and I only admined
the systems to the point I could get going on doing a port).
Solaris and HP-UX (reminder - much less time spent with them) 
seemed, forgive me, disjointed and almost haphazard in their 
organization.

If you want non-propietary, I think you need to look at the BSD
and Linux variants - Linux certainly runs on all the major 
hardware - BSD most (RS6K??) - but for serious scientific work
(read - floating point), many will tell you there is nothing like
Alpha hardware....8-)

It is still on my (ever growing!) list to get my personal copy
of True64 set up on my Alpha after upgrading my Linux do be able
to do some more indepth and current comparisons.....RSN, really.

I am sure others will have more valuable thoughts to offer, but
perhaps you could be more specific about what you want to do, and
folks could give more specific help?

Cheers.




