> Some others UNICEs are reported to be a little proprietry (ie AIX).
> 
> I am requiring advices of people who have used Tru64 and others UNICES.

All UNIX are proprietary.  You can only but Solaris from Sun, Tru64 from
Compaq, HP-UX from HP, AIX from IBM, IRIX from SGI, ...

Consider the vendor's point of view on your question, what is the vendor
going to do to get you to buy his UNIX over anyone elses?  Add something
so a claim can be made that his UNIX is better.  That makes all UNIX 
different:
   to end the user all UNIX are about the same
   to the programmer all UNIX are a bit different
   to the system admin, all UNIX are greatly different

Linux never claimed to be UNIX (the claim is a POSIX compliant OS, but
then so was VMS, MPE, MVS, ...), but since it looks like UNIX in many
ways and so much UNIX software runs on it, it's your real choice in a 
non-proprietary OS.  And it's sold by several vendors each of which 
adds something, sound familiar?

If you are running VMS on an Alpha you can run Tru64 on the same
hardware, which can represent a savings.  If your happy and comfortable
working with Compaq, you can keep that relationship.  If you detest 
working with Compaq, here's a chance to let them know.

If you are happy and comfortable working with VMS, this could be time to
find a new job.

