>else this afternoon. I put in the CD and tried to boot the cdrom in single
>user mode. However when I got into the shell, the "/" partition is read
>only. Well.. in fact, even though it is read and write enabled, I don't
>think I know how to bring it back up.
>
>Could someone tell me what I should do? Is the kernel got corrupted? How
>would I regenerate the new kernel again when "/" is read only?

Yes, your kernel is corrupted (very corrupted).
I assume the message is:

	no vmunix module_list

This means the the fil you are trying to boot is no longer an
executable, but a plain text file. This probably should not happen.
I don't know how it got damaged, or why plugging a tape drive in
should have made anything happen.

There should be a /genvmunix on your system, boot that and you will
be all right. If it isn't on your system, try the following:

	>>> boot -fl g

