In article <8cf3c9$cen$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, Subba <bsubbudu@my-deja.com>
wrote:
> I have two AdvFS filesystems A and B as follows:
>
> A:
>  root_domain is the domain
>  root        is the fileset
>
> B:
>  root_domain is the domain
>  root        is the fileset
>
> Both are bootable partitions only. If I boot through any of them (lets
>say A), how do I mount root_domain#root of B on A? Is that possible? I
>think I am asking a basic question, but I don't find any documentation
>regarding this :(

When A is booted, run the command 

        /sbin/advfs/advscan -r rzXX

where rzXX is the device containing system B
("man 8 advscan" for more information)

This will find all the domains on rzXX and create domain directories of
the form /etc/fdmns/domain_rzXXa,  /etc/fdmns/domain_rzXXg, etc...

Since the domain name is just the name of the directory in /etc/fdmns,
you can use the mv command to change domain_rzXXa to anything you like. 
For example:

        mv /etc/fdmns/domain_rzXXa /etc/fdmns/system_B

Then mount the domain:

        mount system_B#root /mnt

When B is booted, just do the same thing for the device that contains
system A.



