> that come with V5.0 won't run on the the V3.2 (or even V4.0) systems
> due to changes to the stat(2) family of calls to make some fields bigger,
> but most of your V3.2 programs will work with V5.0.

Yes, but Philip wants a dual boot Alpha400 and that's also very easy.
Add the new disc, determine its address using 'sho dev' at the P00>>>
boot prompt. 
Install the new version of Unix entirely on the new disc.

Now, say the original disk is dka0 and the new disc is dka100,
then he can halt the machine and either type
boot dka100
or
boot dka0 
at the P00>>> prompt.

From Unix you could use the consvar command to change the value
of the default boot device: bootdef_dev
unix> consvar -s bootdef_dev rz#
unix> consvar -a
then a 'reboot' or 'shutdown -r' command will 
boot the machine using the new value of bootdef_dev.  Note that
consvar uses rz style disc names: rz0, rz1, etc., rather than
disc controller style names dka200, dkb400, etc.

HTH

-- 
Jan



